00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.640
The way this is gonna work, um we're gonna have a&nbsp;
race between prime numbers, okay. And we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.700
to have two different categories: those which are&nbsp;
1 mod 4, meaning when you divide by 4 they leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.200
a remainder of 1, and those which are 3 mod&nbsp;
4. And we're basically just going to count up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.500
the prime numbers. So the first one is 2, and 2 is&nbsp;
disqualified from the race because it's the oddest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.640
prime, it doesn't really fit into the pattern of&nbsp;
the rest, it's the only one that's even. Next uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:53.280
we've got 3 and then the next one is 5 and then&nbsp;
the next prime is 7. 7 is 3 above a multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:00.300
of 4; and then 11 is also 3 above a multiple of&nbsp;
4. And then the next one 13 is just 1 above a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:06.180
multiple of 4, and then the next one, 17. So they&nbsp;
seem pretty neck and neck so far as we're kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:12.600
counting up. So after 17 we've got 19, which is&nbsp;
3 above a multiple of 4, and then 23 which is 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.600
above a multiple of 4. And then the next one will&nbsp;
be 29, and then after that we're going to have 31,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.340
37. And one thing you might notice is that&nbsp;
so far in the whole race, even though it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.540
pretty neck and neck, team 3 has always been&nbsp;
in the lead so far. And you might say, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.100
that seems maybe just kind of at random that that&nbsp;
would be the case; but actually if we continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.470
this race - and if you want I can give you some&nbsp;
visuals that like show the race continuing on -&nbsp;

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.790
- (Brady: Do you know someone) (who's&nbsp;
good at making mathematics visuals?)&nbsp;

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:43.680
- I I can&nbsp;call a guy. But uh&nbsp;
it will be- it'll always be 3&nbsp;

