00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:03.840
a conjecture that someone made that said: is&nbsp;
it the case that as we let n tend to infinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.700
and we consider at all of the points of the&nbsp;
race, how often was it the case that team 3&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.680
was in the lead? Does that percentage of the&nbsp;
time approach a 100%? Interesting question.&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.760
You might expect it to be true, if there's&nbsp;
any reason that there should be more 3s then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.860
you might expect it to either fall, you know,&nbsp;
completely 50/50 or completely to 100%. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.680
you know, I think the question I'd like&nbsp;
to talk about a little is why would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.880
expect something like this to happen?
It feels like the prime shouldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.300
a bias one way or another, um why does&nbsp;
this team get any kind of advantage?&nbsp;

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.780
- (Well primes do have a bias, they&nbsp;
have a bias towards odd numbers )

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.320
Fair point, okay, so there's a very&nbsp;
strong bias towards odd numbers;&nbsp;

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.840
um and maybe you're on to something there that we&nbsp;
shouldn't treat primes so randomly. But often you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.860
have this criteria that's once you rule out&nbsp;
the obvious things that primes aren't going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:54.480
to do, the things based on divisibility&nbsp;
constraints, their behavior feels random.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.040
And this this heuristic actually helps people&nbsp;
make a lot of conjectures and a lot of it kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.860
of susses out- uh turns out to be true once&nbsp;
you delve into it. And so something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.720
this- another one um that's in in same vein,
let's say we look at the last digits of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.780
primes. Is it going to be a 1? Is it going&nbsp;
to be a 3? Is it going to be a 7 or is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

