going to be a 9? If you perform that race, as&nbsp;
you do that longer and longer they all tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:23.580
be about 25% of the time, but two of them are&nbsp;
distinguished winners. They tend to be winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.420
the race even if it's just by a little bit. So&nbsp;
in the long run it tends to be about 25% percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.880
of them primes end in a 1 but there is just this&nbsp;
gentle bias. And you might ask why; okay there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.840
a very- you say the biased to be odd, there's a&nbsp;
very clear reason they're like strongly biased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.400
to be odd. What is the what is the reason then&nbsp;
that 3 mod 4 would have this kind of advantage?&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.300
- (You have- so are you) (confirming to me&nbsp;
that it does have an advantage? Is it- has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:50.160
that conjecture been proven or?) That's- that's&nbsp;
a really interesting question which we will get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.880
to at the end of the video actually. That- for&nbsp;
the moment let's just consider that conjecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:00.120
something that has strong empirical suggestiveness&nbsp;
um but it's very tricky to prove that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:05.040
thing and then the answer is surprising and we&nbsp;
will get to it. Ah, here's a question of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.120
much detail we want to go into. I think this is&nbsp;
a pretty satisfying intuition, if you're willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.000
to take on faith one magical fact that I present&nbsp;
to you. So the story is going to start outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.220
here but instead with a formula a lot of people&nbsp;
might know for pi of all things: 1 over 1 minus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:27.300
1 over 3 plus 1 over 5 minus 1 over 7... So I'm&nbsp;
basically toggling whether I add or subtract,&nbsp;

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.220
and I'm going over all of the odd numbers.&nbsp;
So this isn't primes, it's all of the odd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:37.200
numbers that I'm doing. In fact I'm going&nbsp;
to give a name to let it be kind of clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.060
this plus minus pattern where I'm going to make&nbsp;
up a function that seems needlessly complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.940
right now that I'm going to call chi of n. And&nbsp;
basically chi of n is going to be defined to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:58.020
+1 if n is 1 mod 4, it's going to be -1 when n&nbsp;
is 3 mod 4, and then I could maybe say that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:03.840
0 you know if n is even. And so basically what&nbsp;
we're doing is we're adding up chi of n divided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.880
by n. Overly fancy way of saying, I'm toggling&nbsp;
between plus and minus on the odd numbers, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.980
it's it's nice to have a name for that function&nbsp;
for what we get to in a moment. Now some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:18.720
might know that this actually converges, if you&nbsp;
look at this on the number line you have your 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.280
you subtract back a little bit to 1/3,
plus 1/5, minus 1/7 - and I can even draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:29.520
that out a little bit if we want. We've got 1,&nbsp;
and if we subtract 1/3 you land somewhere here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:37.860
if we add 1/5, you know, because that step is&nbsp;
smaller we don't quite get to that 1. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:43.140
if we subtract 1/7 that step was smaller still and&nbsp;
so you'll basically be bouncing back and forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:48.300
You know you're going to converge to something&nbsp;
and the value you converge to is pi-fourths. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:52.620
is actually not important for where we're going&nbsp;
but the fact that it's a number- it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.660
be something. Who doesn't love pi right? Right now&nbsp;
pi is almost just like an imposter into our story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:02.460
it doesn't really have much of importance other&nbsp;
than the fact that it's something. Now the magical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:07.560
fact, the absolute magical fact, is the number&nbsp;
theorists favorite way to take a logarithm.&nbsp;

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:14.160
And it works like this, we're going to take our&nbsp;
series - and in order for this trick to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.420
there's just a couple criteria the series has&nbsp;
to have. There's got to be a number associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:24.000
with every natural number, so we might think of 0s&nbsp;
for all of the evens here, um and then it has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.520
have the property that when I multiply the terms&nbsp;
associated with two numbers like 3 and 5 that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.040
product of the terms corresponds to the product&nbsp;
of those numbers. So in this case 3 times 5 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:42.780
15 and negative 1/3 times positive 1/5 is indeed&nbsp;
negative 1/15. It's not exactly obvious but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:49.200
will hold no matter which ones you look at. So for&nbsp;
example if we take 1/7 and 1/3: negative 1/7 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:56.400
negative 1/3 will be positive 1/21. And if we go-&nbsp;
it would be a positive 1/21. So as long as that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.800
property holds you can do this magical thing&nbsp;
to take a logarithm, where I'm going to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.580
I don't like composite numbers, I'm going to kick&nbsp;
out my composite numbers. If you're not prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.260
um either you're not going to be in the club or&nbsp;
you're going to get a strong punishment based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.760
on how not prime you are. So 1 we don't&nbsp;
like you, 1 gets kicked out entirely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:20.216
1/3 you get to stay. So your term&nbsp;
gets to stay, 1/5 you get to stay,

00:08:20.216 --> 00:08:27.180
1/7 you get to stay. 1/9 we say okay,&nbsp;
1/9 you're not prime, I don't like you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.800
but you're just a prime power. At least you&nbsp;
only have one prime factor so you're not as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:37.020
bad as those dirty composites like 6 or 15. So&nbsp;
we'll let you stay, but because you're the square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:42.480
of a prime we're going to give you a punishment&nbsp;
by reducing you by 1/2. So it gets to stay but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.520
only half as powerful. And then it goes on: 1/11,&nbsp;
you're prime, you're cool, you get to stay. 1/13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.160
you're prime, you're cool, you get to stay. 1/15,&nbsp;
not only are you not prime you have two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.240
prime factors, you're very composite, you're out&nbsp;
of the club entirely. And I'll just do a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:02.040
more to kind of make it clear: we've got 1/17 -&nbsp;
and notice I've got two pluses in a row but that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.260
because we kind of kicked out the minus. All we&nbsp;
care about is whether this number is 1 mod 4 or 3

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:14.340
mod 4. So I'll be subtracting off 1/19,&nbsp;
I will also be subtracting off 1/23,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:22.920
because uh 1/21 got kicked out for having too many&nbsp;
factors. 1/25 we're going to have to keep him but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.320
divide him by 2. A way of saying, okay you're not&nbsp;
prime, but you're just a prime power, and because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.340
you're the square of a prime you get reduced. And&nbsp;
just to make this extra clear, the next one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:40.800
we see is the cube of a prime, 1/27, so I'm going&nbsp;
to reduce it by 1/3 that gets me 1/ 27. In general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:48.480
what we're doing here is we're taking 1 over k&nbsp;
times something divided by p to the power of k;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.660
where p is a prime number, k could be anything,&nbsp;
and we're basically taking this chi function of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.660 --> 00:10:01.200
p to the k. Which is just a way of measuring, are&nbsp;
you plu- are you 1 mod 4 or 3 mod 4? So that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.020
be how you write this in general.
- (So Grant if this is)

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.540
(some kind of shortcut, it's not&nbsp;
feeling like a shortcut but all right)&nbsp;

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.720
- So it's it's not a shortcut,
it's just the coolest way-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:13.680
because if you- okay I do this, I do this&nbsp;
completely random procedure to my series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.040
and you'd say what is it going to equal?
I don't know, some completely different number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.240
that's completely unrelated to the thing that we&nbsp;
started with. Because you went, primes are such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.260
a random thing that you're hacking them off at&nbsp;
these seemingly random points, you should expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:30.720
nothing normal to come from this. But the beauty&nbsp;
is that what you get is just the natural log of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.400
whatever it was before. And um we could do this&nbsp;
with a lot of different things actually: another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:45.120
famous sequence people might know is if we take 1&nbsp;
over 1 + 1 over 4 + 1 over over 9 and we add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.440
up the reciprocals of all the square numbers. So&nbsp;
we kind of go on and we're always adding up 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:55.260
over n squared. This has the property we care about&nbsp;
where when you multiply corresponding terms, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.120
you get corresponds to their product. If we do the&nbsp;
same game, we kick out some prime numbers, we keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.820
the prime powers but we reduce them a little bit,&nbsp;
uh it'll again be a logarithm. So this sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:11.940
happens to be pi squared divided by 6, and if we do&nbsp;
our whole like prime number game - whatever we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:18.060
to call it - what we'll get will be the natural log&nbsp;
of pi squared over 6. And so that that holds in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:23.400
general and it's it's the weirdest possible way&nbsp;
to take a logarithm. Why am I talking about this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:30.960
The reason is that if we look at the sequence&nbsp;
that we have here; now we have uh mostly primes,&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.880
we've got some prime powers in there, but it's&nbsp;
mostly prime numbers and we're kind of adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:40.620
or subtracting them and this might give us a&nbsp;
little bit of an indication that if they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:45.060
to stay roughly evenly balanced, you know, that&nbsp;
would justify the fact that we're equaling some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:50.040
constant. Where if there were systematically way&nbsp;
more that are 1 above a multiple of 4 or way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:54.660
more that are 3 above a multiple of 4, the whole&nbsp;
series might diverge. A couple other magical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.660 --> 00:12:01.020
facts I'll throw down will be that this series&nbsp;
diverges absolutely, which is a way of saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:05.160
if we changed all of the minuses to be pluses&nbsp;
and we just asked, hey I'm going to add up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:09.300
sizes of all of these terms, does that converge&nbsp;
to any number? It goes and blows up to infinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:15.060
And what that kind of tells us is if we look at&nbsp;
the camp that are all of the things that are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:19.560
added and all the camp that are being subtracted,&nbsp;
both of those blow up to infinity. And so when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.520
we're cutting it off at a finite point they have&nbsp;
to stay roughly balanced with each other. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:29.160
tells us two important things: one is why we have a&nbsp;
kind of even balance in our race, and it's not that-

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:33.420
(But this was- but you did this with the&nbsp;
odd numbers, this is just prime numbers but it's)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:38.280
(the same sort of principle)
- The key equation at&nbsp;
this point is to focus on what happens after we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:43.800
do our log trick where all the stuff that remains&nbsp;
in the series is mostly primes. So what I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:49.800
say is, if we kick out all the prime powers&nbsp;
those- they don't actually amount to that much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:56.640
They converge absolutely, um and in fact they&nbsp;
have to be just bounded by some constant. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.080
we could say if we ignore all the prime powers&nbsp;
we would have the sequence which sometimes you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:06.480
adding a prime, sometimes you're subtracting it, and&nbsp;
because it equals just a constant, just some number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:12.360
we can know that neither one of those camps blows&nbsp;
up much faster than the other. However, all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:16.620
nuance for who's winning the race happens when&nbsp;
we really think hard about what happens to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:22.680
prime powers. Here's the like intuition - it is not&nbsp;
a proof - but here's the vague intuition for why you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:27.900
might expect 3 mod 4 to be winning this race even&nbsp;
if you didn't start performing the race. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.400
knew some analytic number theory, you had a strong&nbsp;
familiarity with the series, your prediction might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:39.240
be I'm going to put my money on horse number&nbsp;
3. And how do I want to draw this? I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.800
kind of have two buckets here; this is going to&nbsp;
be all of the stuff that we're adding in our sum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:48.840
and then this is going to be all of the stuff that&nbsp;
we're subtracting. And we're just going to kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:53.220
of- what kind of things are we adding? What kind of&nbsp;
things are we subtracting? So the stuff that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:59.400
adding, it's all of the primes which are 1 above&nbsp;
a multiple of 4 so- or rather the reciprocals of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:05.640
those. So 1/5, 1/13, 1/17 - 
these are the kind of things that we're adding. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:10.920
there's also some squares of primes that we're&nbsp;
adding: in fact critically the square of every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:15.900
prime. Because even if you started from someone who&nbsp;
is going to be a negative, like -1/3, 

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:19.800
once we look at the square term corresponding&nbsp;
